.TH SPACE 9
.SH NAME
space \- an address space per process, and the segments that own its frames
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "kernel:mem"
.PP
.nf
.B "space_new :: proc () -> (^Address_Space, Error)"
.B "space_destroy :: proc (space: ^Address_Space)"
.B "space_switch :: proc (space: ^Address_Space)"
.B "map_user :: proc (space: ^Address_Space, virt, phys: uintptr, flags: arch.Page_Flags, pages: int) -> Error"
.B "protect_user :: proc (space: ^Address_Space, virt: uintptr, pages: int, flags: arch.Page_Flags) -> Error"
.B "remap_user :: proc (space: ^Address_Space, virt, phys: uintptr, flags: arch.Page_Flags) -> Error"
.B "unmap_user :: proc (space: ^Address_Space, virt: uintptr, pages: int) -> Error"
.B "shoot :: proc (root: uintptr, virt: uintptr, pages: int)"
.B "space_stats :: proc () -> Space_Stats"
.fi
.PP
.B import "kernel:user"
.PP
.nf
.B "segment_new :: proc (p: ^Process, kind: Segment_Kind, ...) -> ^Segment"
.B "segment_incref :: proc (s: ^Segment)"
.B "segment_release :: proc (s: ^Segment)"
.B "segment_frame :: proc (s: ^Segment, page: int) -> uintptr"
.B "segment_covering :: proc (p: ^Process, virt: uintptr) -> ^Segment"
.B "map_reserve :: proc (p: ^Process, pages: int) -> (va: uintptr, ok: bool)"
.B "segment_stats :: proc () -> Segment_Stats"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
An
.B Address_Space
in
.B kernel/mem/space.odin
is one page table tree per process.
Its top-level table has 512 entries; the low 256 are a fresh, empty lower
half, and the high 256 are a copy, entry for entry, of the kernel's own
top-level table.
Because the copy is by entry and every space's high half names the same
second-level tables, a kernel mapping made after a space is created is
visible in every space without being replayed into any of them.
The
.B User
permission bit is never set on any of the 256 kernel-half entries, which
is what seals the kernel from a program at the top of the tree, once,
rather than by leaving the bit clear on every mapping anyone makes.
.PP
.B USER_MIN
is one page above address zero, so a null pointer always faults rather
than reading whatever a program put at the bottom of its space.
.B USER_MAX
is where the non-canonical hole starts; an address at or above it is
refused by name rather than by the encoding underneath.
.SS What a space owns
A space owns its lower-half page tables and frees them on
.BR space_destroy .
It does not own the frames those tables' leaves point at.
.B space_destroy
walks only as far as the halfway index and stops: the upper 256 entries
name tables the kernel and every other space still use, and freeing one
would take the kernel down with the process that exited.
.PP
The frame owner is the
.I segment
.RB ( kernel/user/segment.odin ),
not the space.
This split exists because a frame mapped into two spaces, as
.I rfork
makes possible, has two owners and needs a count.
The division:
.IP
.EX
the space       owns its page tables, frees them, never touches a leaf
the segment      owns the frames, frees them at the last release
the process      holds references to both
.EE
.SS Segments
A
.B Segment
is the frames behind one contiguous mapping, refcounted, of one
.BR Segment_Kind :
.BR Text ,
shared by every fork because nothing can write it;
.BR Data ,
copied on
.I rfork
unless
.B RFMEM
shares it;
.BR Stack ,
always a private copy at the same address, because the child continues
its parent's stack;
.BR Device ,
memory the allocator never owned and must never free, such as the
framebuffer, shared unconditionally because there is one piece of
hardware behind it;
and
.BR Anon ,
memory a process asked for by the page through
.B segalloc
rather than by a file, owned and freed like
.B Data
and treated as
.B Data
by
.IR rfork .
.B Shared
is a sixth kind kept across
.IR exec .
A segment is described one of two ways.
.B Text ,
.B Data
and
.B Stack
carry a list of frame numbers, one per page, filled in as pages arrive
and holding zero for a hole a fault fills.
.B Device
and
.B Anon
carry a base and an extent instead, because a thousand-frame device
mapping does not fit a list bounded at
.BR MAX_PROGRAM_FRAMES .
.B segment_is_run
is the one predicate that tells the two shapes apart; every other
operation on a segment treats all kinds alike.
A process holds at most
.B MAX_PROC_SEGS
segments.
.SS Copy-on-write
A
.B Data
or
.B Text
segment shared under
.I rfork
without
.B RFMEM
is mapped read-only in both processes with its reference count raised by
.BR segment_incref .
A write fault to such a page reaches
.B kernel:user's
.BR fix_fault :
if the segment's reference count is still above one, it copies the page,
calls
.B remap_user
to point the faulting process at the copy, and
.B segment_release
drops the shared segment's share of that page.
A write fault to a page the process already owns alone is not copied,
only reprotected.
.SS map_reserve
.B map_reserve
finds the lowest free span in a process's own segment list without
mapping anything.
Both
.B segattach
and
.B segalloc
take their address from it, so the two calls share one search and their
ranges cannot overlap.
A span
.B segdetach
frees becomes a hole the next call can land in, and a child inherits its
parent's list along with its address space, so its first allocation
after
.I rfork
lands clear of everything the parent already held.
An anonymous segment is bounded by
.BR SEGALLOC_MAX ,
four megabytes.
.SS The shootdown
.B invlpg
drops a translation on the core that runs it, and no other.
A core translating through the same space keeps a stale entry until it
is told to drop it.
.B unmap_user
calls
.B shoot
after it changes the tables, which asks every core whose current thread
runs in the affected space, or every core if the kernel's own space
changed, to drop the range and count itself off; the caller waits for
the count to reach zero.
.B unmap_user_quiet
takes the entries out without shooting, for a caller that must change
several spaces under one lock and cannot wait on other cores while
holding it; that caller calls
.B shoot
once for each space after the lock is released, and the frames involved
may not be reused before that.
One shootdown is in flight at a time.
The sender may hold no spinlock and may not be a handler, because a
receiver spinning on a lock the sender holds would never take the
interrupt that lets it answer; see
.IR sync (9).
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/mem/space.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/segment.odin
